1737:If $(z_1,z_2)=(2,2)$, then $S^{\pr}S^{\pr(-1)}=3((0,0)+(1,2)+(2,1))$. Therefore, $SS^{(-1)}$ equals three copies of an order three subgroup of $\Z_3 \times \Z_3$, which means $S$ is not primitive by Lemma~\ref{lem-priset}. In the remaining cases, it is easy to verify that $S^{\pr}$, and hence $S$, is a $(3,3,3,1)$-RDS in $G$.

